WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.580
Integrins connect cells to the extracellular matrix, the woven material cells

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.880
secrete around themselves.

3
00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.000
Inside, they link to the cytoskeleton and send signals.

4
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:18.040
So this grip lets a pull outside reach the cell's own filaments.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.340
Each integrin pairs an α subunit with a β subunit.

6
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.460
Many recognise RGD in fibronectin.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.140
Talin binds the β tail and links it to actin.

8
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.500
Vinculin reinforces the link.

9
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:36.280
These complexes gather into focal adhesions beneath cultured cells.

10
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.680
Not every integrin reaches actin.

11
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.100
Hemidesmosomes pin basal skin cells to their basement membrane.

12
00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.860
Integrin α6β4 binds laminin-332 outside.

13
00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.700
Inside, it connects through plectin to keratin filaments.

14
00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:57.313
The other grip joins neighbouring cells.

15
00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:00.893
Cadherins need calcium to stiffen their outer arms.

16
00:01:01.753 --> 00:01:05.293
Remove calcium from cultured epithelial cells and they let go.

17
00:01:06.153 --> 00:01:07.933
Put it back and they knit together again.

18
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:14.097
A classical cadherin crosses the membrane once and often binds the same kind on the

19
00:01:14.097 --> 00:01:19.837
next cell. Inside, beta β-catenin binds its tail and recruits alpha α-catenin, which

20
00:01:19.837 --> 00:01:21.377
couples the junction to actin.

21
00:01:22.237 --> 00:01:24.577
These contacts gather into adherens junctions.

22
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.280
What does the matrix contribute?

23
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:29.780
Collagen cables resist pulling.

24
00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:32.280
Elastic fibres stretch and recoil.

25
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.300
Fibronectin makes networks that cells grip.

26
00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:40.780
Basement membranes are thin sheets built from linked laminin and type IV collagen

27
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:46.180
networks. Proteoglycans draw in water, forming gels that resist compression.

28
00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.340
Hyaluronan helps keep the matrix hydrated.

29
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:54.500
The matrix also stores growth factors, which can be released when it is cut or

30
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.780
pulled. It supplies mechanical support and biochemical cues.

31
00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:02.650
Some of these connections also respond to force.

32
00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.030
A cell pulls on its adhesions with myosin II.

33
00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:10.550
Stretching purified talin exposes hidden vinculin-binding sites.

34
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:16.890
In a reconstituted cadherin-catenin complex, some bonds to actin last longer under

35
00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.790
load, over a limited range.

36
00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.010
Matrix mechanics can also bias cell behaviour in culture.

37
00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.450
Adhesions connect the matrix and neighbouring cells to internal filaments, and some

38
00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.570
links change when pulled.
